              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Chandragupta Maurya</strong><br>\nChandragupta Maurya's origins are shrouded in mystery, but ancient texts suggest he was born into a family of humble means. His early life was marked by his encounter with the shrewd Brahmin Chanakya, who would become his mentor. Chanakya, struck by Chandragupta's leadership qualities, trained him in the art of governance and warfare. This training prepared Chandragupta to challenge the powerful Nanda dynasty that ruled much of Northern India at the time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Fall of the Nanda Dynasty</strong><br>\nUnder Chanakya's guidance, Chandragupta raised an army and pursued his ambition to overthrow the Nanda dynasty. The Nandas were unpopular due to their oppressive rule, making it easier for Chandragupta to gain support. Through a combination of warfare and alliances, Chandragupta was able to dethrone the last Nanda king. His victory marked the end of Nanda power and the beginning of the Mauryan era.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Establishment of the Maurya Empire</strong><br>\nAfter defeating the Nandas, Chandragupta Maurya continued to expand his territory. He conquered much of the Indian subcontinent, which led to the formation of one of the largest empires in India at that time. His empire stretched from the Bay of Bengal to the Arabian Sea and from the Himalayas to the Deccan Plateau. The administrative system he established was highly advanced and was a model for subsequent rulers.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
