            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Consolidation of Power</strong><br>\nChandragupta's consolidation of power was impressive. He centralized the administration and implemented a sophisticated system of bureaucracy with a network of spies and officials that reported directly to the emperor. This system helped maintain control over a vast and diverse empire. He also standardized weights and measures, developed trade, and promoted agriculture, significantly boosting the empire's economy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Relations with Alexander the Great</strong><br>\nChandragupta Maurya came to power shortly after Alexander the Great's incursion into India. The retreat of Alexander's army left a power vacuum in the northwest region of the subcontinent. Chandragupta seized this opportunity to liberate the Indian territories from the Macedonian governors left behind and expanded his dominion into these areas, which increased his empire's resources and influence.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Economic Policies</strong><br>\nThe economic policies of Chandragupta were foundational to the empire's prosperity. He regulated trade routes, protected commerce from banditry, and established a uniform tax system across the empire. The Mauryan economy thrived on agriculture, with the state overseeing irrigation projects to increase agricultural productivity. The wealth collected from these efforts helped fund Chandragupta's military campaigns and administrative expenses.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
