          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Military Strength and Strategies</strong><br>\nChandragupta Maurya's military prowess was central to his success. He maintained a large standing army, which included a cavalry, infantry, chariots, and war elephants. Chanakya's treatise, the Arthashastra, provides insights into the military strategies employed by Chandragupta, such as the use of spies, and the importance of securing and fortifying territories to prevent rebellions and invasions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Social and Religious Life</strong><br>\nThe Mauryan Empire under Chandragupta was characterized by a diverse social structure. Society was divided into a variety of professions and trades, which was reflected in urban development. Religious life was also diverse, with Hinduism, Buddhism, and Jainism coexisting. Chandragupta eventually adopted Jainism, and it is said that he abdicated his throne to live as an ascetic in his later years.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nChandragupta Maurya's death is as debated as his life, with various accounts of his end. Some say he died following a strict Jain ritual of fasting, while others suggest different causes. His legacy, however, is clear and enduring. He established a regime that lasted for over a century and laid the foundations for his grandson Ashoka, who would become one of India's most legendary rulers. The Maurya Empire set a standard for governance and empire-building in Indian history.</p>\n"
              },
